const rows = [
['d1-t01',
'An audit charter establishes the audit function’s authority, responsibilities and access. Independence supports objective judgment; auditors may advise on controls while management remains accountable for decisions and operation.',
'A system owner asks the auditor to approve production access before later auditing those approvals.',
'Confusing advisory involvement with permission to own the control you will evaluate.',
'Who owns the control, and what would compromise the auditor’s objectivity?',
'An auditor will later assess a new access workflow. Which role best preserves objectivity?',
'Recommend control criteria while management approves and operates the workflow.',
'Approve each user’s production access.','Accept responsibility for removing leavers.','Operate the workflow and audit the same transactions.',
'Advice can help management design controls without transferring management’s responsibility to the auditor.'],
['d1-t02',
'An audit evaluates evidence against defined criteria. Its purpose determines the work: compliance asks whether obligations are met, operational work examines effectiveness and efficiency, and financial-related work considers reporting risks. An assessment is not automatically an assurance opinion.',
'A team checks compliance with an access policy and separately investigates whether support staffing meets service needs.',
'Choosing an audit method because it is familiar before defining the objective.',
'How would the objective change your criteria and evidence?',
'Before choosing tests for an unfamiliar review, what should the auditor establish?',
'The review objective, scope and evaluation criteria.',
'The largest available data extract.','The preferred testing tool.','The control owner’s desired conclusion.',
'The objective and criteria explain what the review is trying to establish and which evidence can answer it.'],
['d1-t03',
'Risk-based planning links limited audit effort to business exposure. Understand objectives, threats, impact, likelihood and controls, then prioritize residual risk. Prior audit timing is an input rather than the sole reason to select an area.',
'A low-impact application was last audited five years ago, while a payment platform has recently changed its access model.',
'Equating the oldest audit date or easiest testing with the highest priority.',
'What business change could make a previously low-risk area important?',
'Which review deserves attention first when resources are limited?',
'The area with the greatest supported residual business risk.',
'The area that supplies evidence fastest.','The area whose manager volunteers first.','The area with the longest control checklist, regardless of impact.',
'Priority follows risk to business objectives, supported by evidence rather than convenience.'],
['d1-t04',
'A preventive control blocks an unwanted event, a detective control identifies it, and a corrective control helps restore an acceptable state. Evaluate design against the risk, then test operation. A compensating control must address the original exposure with adequate precision.',
'A small team cannot fully separate access administration and review, so an independent manager reviews privileged changes daily.',
'Calling any extra review a compensating control without assessing coverage and timeliness.',
'What exact failure would this control prevent or detect?',
'What best supports accepting an independent review as a compensating control?',
'It addresses the same risk with suitable coverage, precision and timing.',
'It uses a more expensive tool.','It is documented as an additional step without assessing its precision.','It is performed by the original administrator.',
'A substitute control must reduce the relevant risk, not merely add an activity.'],
['d1-t05',
'Audit project management coordinates scope, staff, skills, evidence requests, milestones and communication. When a significant new risk appears, assess its effect and obtain appropriate scope or resource decisions rather than quietly abandoning planned work.',
'Halfway through an access audit, the team discovers a major migration that changes the population being tested.',
'Expanding scope informally until the team cannot meet any objective.',
'How would you escalate a scope change while protecting the audit objective?',
'A major unplanned risk threatens the audit timeline. What is the best next step?',
'Assess the impact and agree an authorized scope or resource adjustment.',
'Ignore it because planning has ended.','Silently remove planned high-risk tests.','Promise the original deadline without reviewing resources.',
'A documented decision makes risk, scope and constraints visible to the right stakeholders.'],
['d1-t06',
'Sampling draws conclusions about a defined population. Attribute sampling evaluates control deviations; variables sampling estimates amounts. Define the population and selection method, verify completeness, and interpret exceptions in context. Greater acceptable risk generally supports less assurance, not more.',
'An auditor samples approved changes but later discovers emergency changes were excluded from the extract.',
'Using a large sample to compensate for a population that omits relevant items.',
'Why can a perfect sample from an incomplete population mislead you?',
'What should be resolved before selecting an audit sample?',
'Whether the population is complete and appropriate to the objective.',
'Whether the first records look reasonable.','Whether every item has the same manager.','Whether a previous audit used the same sample size.',
'Sampling cannot provide the intended coverage when relevant population items are absent.'],
['d1-t07',
'Evidence must be sufficient in quantity and appropriate in relevance and reliability. Inquiry explains a process but often needs corroboration. Reperformance, observation, documents and independent sources answer different questions. Preserve the evidence trail so another reviewer can follow the conclusion.',
'A control owner says every terminated account is disabled promptly, but provides no records.',
'Treating a confident explanation as proof that the control operated throughout the period.',
'What corroborating evidence would convert this claim into a supported conclusion?',
'Which procedure most directly tests whether a selected leaver account was disabled on time?',
'Compare reliable termination and account-disable timestamps.',
'Ask the manager if the process is reliable.','Read only the current access policy.','Count the total number of user accounts.',
'Timely operation is supported by relevant transaction evidence rather than intent alone.'],
['d1-t08',
'Data analytics can examine whole populations for patterns or exceptions, but the result depends on reliable inputs and sound logic. Reconcile data, validate joins and filters, and investigate exceptions. A match or anomaly is a lead rather than an automatic finding.',
'A duplicate-payment query flags transactions, but a join has multiplied rows from a vendor history table.',
'Assuming automated output is correct because the query ran successfully.',
'How could you validate the input and transformation before interpreting the output?',
'An analysis produces a surprising surge of exceptions. What should happen before reporting a finding?',
'Validate the data and analysis logic, then investigate the exceptions.',
'Label every exception fraud.','Remove outliers until the chart looks normal.','Assume a successful query proves data quality.',
'A sound conclusion requires reliable source data, correct logic and contextual investigation.'],
['d1-t09',
'A useful finding connects criteria, condition, cause and effect or risk. Communicate significance in business terms, confirm factual accuracy, and identify accountable action owners and dates. Management can disagree, but unsupported optimism should not replace the auditor’s judgment.',
'A report lists unreviewed privileged accounts but does not explain the risk or required response.',
'Overloading a finding with technical detail while obscuring business impact.',
'How would you explain this condition to someone who does not administer the system?',
'Which addition makes an access finding most useful to senior management?',
'The business exposure and an accountable remediation plan.',
'A longer list of command syntax.','The auditor’s preferred product brand.','A statement that the administrator is careless.',
'A clear risk and action plan help decision makers understand and address the exposure.'],
['d1-t10',
'Audit quality assurance evaluates whether audit work follows applicable standards and produces supported, useful conclusions. Supervision, workpaper review, feedback and improvement measures support consistent quality. Measure the work’s effectiveness as well as its speed.',
'A department finishes every audit on time, but several conclusions lack evidence.',
'Using completion rate alone as proof of audit quality.',
'What evidence would show that a conclusion can withstand independent review?',
'Which quality measure best complements on-time audit completion?',
'Independent review of evidence supporting key conclusions.',
'The number of low-risk details included in the report.','The percentage of findings accepted without discussion.','The number of checklist steps completed without reviewing their purpose.',
'Timeliness matters, but conclusions also need adequate evidence and sound professional judgment.'],

['d2-t01',
'Identify the obligations that apply to the organization, its activities and its data before assessing compliance. Map requirements to accountable owners, processes and controls. A standard may guide practice without automatically being a legal requirement for every organization.',
'A company enters a new market and begins processing a different category of customer data.',
'Applying a familiar regulation without checking jurisdiction, scope or applicability.',
'What must be understood before you can define compliance criteria?',
'What should guide the initial compliance scope for a new business activity?',
'The obligations applicable to that activity and its data.',
'Only the requirements already covered by the current control library.','Whatever controls a competitor advertises.','The shortest available compliance checklist.',
'Applicability establishes the requirements against which compliance should be assessed.'],
['d2-t02',
'Governance sets direction, evaluates stakeholder needs and monitors outcomes; management plans and executes within that direction. IT strategy should support enterprise objectives. Decision rights and accountability need to be clear even when services are outsourced.',
'An IT team selects a major platform without a business sponsor or agreed enterprise outcome.',
'Confusing a detailed technology roadmap with evidence of business alignment.',
'Who sets direction, who executes it, and how is value monitored?',
'What provides the strongest basis for an IT strategy?',
'Approved enterprise objectives and stakeholder needs.',
'The newest vendor roadmap alone.','The technology architecture preferred by the IT team, without business input.','The largest available technology budget.',
'Technology decisions should support agreed business direction rather than become an end in themselves.'],
['d2-t03',
'Policies set management’s intent and requirements, standards define consistent mandatory rules, procedures describe actions, and guidelines provide advice. Keep the hierarchy approved, current, communicated and enforceable. Exceptions need a documented risk decision and expiry or review.',
'Teams follow different password practices because a high-level policy has no supporting standard.',
'Expecting a broad policy sentence to provide every operational instruction.',
'Which document would set the mandatory baseline, and which would explain the steps?',
'Where should a mandatory, organization-wide configuration baseline normally be defined?',
'In an approved supporting standard.',
'In an optional suggestion list.','Only in one engineer’s notes.','Only in an unapproved team-specific procedure.',
'Standards translate policy intent into consistent mandatory requirements.'],
['d2-t04',
'Enterprise architecture relates business capabilities, information, applications and technology. It helps assess dependencies, integration and the effect of change. Evaluate whether architecture decisions serve the enterprise rather than only one team’s immediate needs.',
'Three departments buy separate customer platforms that cannot exchange consistent records.',
'Optimizing a local application while overlooking enterprise data and integration consequences.',
'What shared capability or dependency should be considered before approving the new system?',
'What is a key benefit of enterprise architecture in an acquisition decision?',
'Understanding fit and dependencies across the business and technology landscape.',
'Guaranteeing that every project uses the same vendor.','Eliminating the need for risk assessment.','Making every technology change an audit decision.',
'Architecture supports informed integration and alignment; it does not replace governance or risk decisions.'],
['d2-t05',
'Risk management identifies, assesses, responds to and monitors risk relative to objectives and appetite. Business management owns risk; auditors evaluate the process. Residual risk remains after controls, and acceptance requires an authorized owner rather than an informal technical workaround.',
'A security engineer wants to accept a material exposure because a remediation is expensive.',
'Letting the control implementer make an acceptance decision beyond their authority.',
'Who can accept this residual risk, and what information would they need?',
'Who should accept a significant residual business risk?',
'The accountable management owner with appropriate authority.',
'The external auditor by default.','Any administrator who understands the server.','The software vendor without business approval.',
'Risk acceptance is a management decision within defined authority and appetite.'],
['d2-t06',
'A privacy program addresses how personal data is collected, used, shared, retained and disposed of. Purpose, minimization, transparency, accountability and applicable individual rights are central considerations. Security protects data, but privacy also asks whether processing is appropriate.',
'A securely encrypted application collects birth dates that its service does not need.',
'Treating encryption as evidence that all privacy concerns have been resolved.',
'What privacy question remains even if unauthorized access is unlikely?',
'Which concern best illustrates data minimization?',
'Whether each collected personal-data field is necessary for a defined purpose.',
'Whether access to the data is protected by authentication.','Whether the system stores the maximum possible history.','Whether encryption makes any collection acceptable.',
'Minimization evaluates necessity and scope of collection, not merely protection after collection.'],
['d2-t07',
'Data governance establishes accountability for data quality, use and life cycle. Business owners determine classification based on value, sensitivity and obligations; custodians implement handling. Classification should drive controls, not be a label disconnected from actual treatment.',
'A database team decides a sales dataset is public without consulting its business owner.',
'Confusing technical custody with authority to decide business sensitivity.',
'Who determines the classification, and who implements the resulting safeguards?',
'Who should normally approve the business classification of a dataset?',
'The accountable business data owner.',
'The database administrator acting without business-owner approval.','Any user able to export it.','The auditor preparing the test sample.',
'The business owner understands the data’s value and requirements; custodians carry out the handling decisions.'],
['d2-t08',
'IT resource management balances people, skills, assets, capacity and funding against business demand. Review whether staffing and dependencies can sustain agreed services. A low cost does not establish adequate capacity, competence or resilience.',
'A critical platform has one administrator and no documented backup coverage.',
'Measuring staffing efficiency while ignoring a key-person dependency.',
'What could interrupt service if this resource becomes unavailable?',
'Which observation most directly indicates a resource continuity risk?',
'Critical knowledge is held by one person without effective backup coverage.',
'The team uses a shared calendar.','The department met its travel budget.','The system has documented support roles and trained backup staff.',
'A single-person dependency can leave essential operations unsupported when that person is unavailable.'],
['d2-t09',
'Vendor management spans selection, contracting, monitoring and exit. Outsourcing execution does not outsource organizational accountability. Contracts should address service, security, data, assurance access and transition needs; monitoring should evaluate actual performance and risk.',
'A processor meets its uptime target but cannot explain how the customer would retrieve data on exit.',
'Considering vendor due diligence complete once the contract is signed.',
'What evidence would show that exit and ongoing assurance are workable?',
'Which responsibility remains with the customer after outsourcing an IT service?',
'Oversight of the service’s business risk and contractual performance.',
'Assuming the vendor has accepted every organizational risk.','Ending all monitoring because the service is external.','Transferring board accountability to the vendor’s help desk.',
'The organization remains accountable for outcomes and needs ongoing vendor oversight.'],
['d2-t10',
'Performance monitoring should show whether IT delivers agreed outcomes. KPIs track performance; KRIs warn about exposure. Measures need clear definitions, reliable data and thresholds that support decisions. Activity volume alone may say little about value or risk.',
'A help desk closes many tickets quickly, but repeat incidents and customer disruption are increasing.',
'Rewarding a volume metric that hides poor service outcomes.',
'Which outcome and risk measures would make this dashboard more informative?',
'What best complements the number of tickets closed?',
'Recurrence, service impact and achievement of agreed response or recovery targets.',
'Only the percentage of tickets closed without independent quality review.','Only the number of help-desk employees.','Only the average number of tickets assigned per technician.',
'Outcome and risk measures reveal whether apparent productivity translates into effective service.'],
['d2-t11',
'Quality management defines expected outcomes and builds assurance and improvement into IT processes. Quality assurance evaluates the process; quality control checks particular outputs. Use defect and outcome information to improve causes rather than only detect mistakes after release.',
'A team repeatedly fixes production defects but never changes the development process that creates them.',
'Calling repeated rework a complete quality-improvement program.',
'Which process change could prevent this recurring defect?',
'Which action best supports continuous quality improvement?',
'Analyze recurring defect causes and improve the underlying process.',
'Report only the total number of patches.','Hide recurring defects from trend reports.','Repeat the same inspection without acting on its results.',
'Improvement uses evidence to change the process responsible for repeated quality problems.'],

['d3-t01',
'Project governance defines sponsorship, accountability, oversight and decisions. Project management coordinates delivery. Risks, scope changes, benefits and dependencies need visible decisions, and stage gates should test meaningful readiness rather than merely confirm a date has arrived.',
'A delivery team skips a risk review to avoid delaying the launch milestone.',
'Equating schedule achievement with project success.',
'Who has authority to trade scope, risk, cost and time?',
'What should a project steering group primarily monitor?',
'Business objectives, significant risks, benefits and major delivery decisions.',
'Only the number of code commits.','Only whether the project has consumed its authorized budget.','Only the number of technical requirements marked complete.',
'Governance focuses on business outcomes and authorized decisions, while detailed execution belongs to project management.'],
['d3-t02',
'A business case compares expected benefits, total costs, risks and alternatives. Feasibility considers whether the proposal can work technically, operationally, economically and within constraints. Keep assumptions and benefit ownership explicit so value can be evaluated after implementation.',
'A proposed automation saves manual effort but requires expensive data cleanup and recurring support.',
'Counting acquisition costs while excluding operation, transition and retirement costs.',
'Which assumption would most change the investment decision?',
'What is most important when evaluating an IT investment’s business case?',
'A credible comparison of total costs, benefits, risks and alternatives.',
'Only the lowest purchase price.','Only the vendor’s demonstration.','The number of features listed in the brochure.',
'An investment decision needs realistic life-cycle economics and business value, not a narrow purchase comparison.'],
['d3-t03',
'Development methods organize how requirements become working systems. Agile uses iterative delivery and feedback; sequential approaches use more defined stages. Whichever method is chosen, requirements, change decisions, testing and security responsibilities still need control.',
'An agile team claims that short sprints mean formal acceptance criteria are unnecessary.',
'Assuming a methodology removes the need for control evidence.',
'How would you demonstrate controlled delivery without imposing an unsuitable process?',
'In an agile project, what still needs to be defined?',
'Acceptance criteria and evidence that delivered changes meet them.',
'A rule that every requirement must never change.','An exemption from all security testing.','A ban on business stakeholder feedback.',
'Iterative delivery changes the rhythm of control activities, not the need to demonstrate suitable outcomes.'],
['d3-t04',
'Identify risks and design controls while requirements and architecture are being developed. Retrofitting controls after go-live can be expensive and incomplete. Business control owners should confirm objectives, and testing should trace requirements through to implemented behavior.',
'A payment application is almost complete when the team first considers approval limits.',
'Waiting until the final audit to define essential application controls.',
'Where would you place the control so it addresses the risk at its source?',
'When should key application control requirements be identified?',
'During requirements and design, before implementation choices become fixed.',
'Only after production losses occur.','Only when an external auditor requests them.','After users have stopped reporting defects.',
'Early control design supports integration with the business process and reduces the cost of late remediation.'],
['d3-t05',
'Readiness testing should address functional requirements, interfaces, security, performance, recovery and user acceptance as relevant. Business users validate that the system supports intended work. Production authorization should consider unresolved defects and documented acceptance.',
'A system passes developer unit tests but business users have not validated the billing process.',
'Using technical test success as a substitute for business acceptance.',
'What evidence would demonstrate that the intended users can perform their work?',
'Who should be accountable for business user acceptance of a new billing process?',
'The authorized business representatives who own the process.',
'Only the developer who wrote the code.','Only the hardware supplier.','The auditor, acting as business management.',
'Business acceptance confirms that the delivered system meets process needs; technical testing alone does not establish that.'],
['d3-t06',
'Release management coordinates approved components into a controlled production deployment. Configuration baselines identify what should be installed; deployment verification confirms what actually changed. Segregation, rollback readiness and traceability reduce implementation risk.',
'A release package includes a configuration file that was never part of the approved test version.',
'Testing one build and deploying a different unverified package.',
'How can you connect the approved build to the deployed configuration?',
'What evidence most directly supports release integrity?',
'Traceability from approved, tested components to the production deployment.',
'A verbal statement that the team was careful.','Only a signed statement that the planned release was installed.','A larger release version number by itself.',
'Traceability helps establish that production received the components evaluated and authorized.'],
['d3-t07',
'Migration converts and transfers systems or data while protecting completeness, accuracy and continuity. Reconcile counts and control totals, validate transformations and exceptions, and preserve fallback options. A successful file transfer does not prove business records were converted correctly.',
'A customer database migration finishes on time, but several account balances were truncated.',
'Using successful job completion as proof of data conversion accuracy.',
'Which reconciliation would reveal missing or altered business records?',
'What should be verified before accepting a material data conversion?',
'Reconciliation of source and target completeness and accuracy.',
'Only that the copy job returned a success code.','Only that the target database is larger.','Only that users can access the target application.',
'Conversion assurance needs evidence that the resulting records preserve the required business information.'],
['d3-t08',
'A postimplementation review compares actual outcomes with the approved business case, requirements and control objectives. Consider benefit realization, residual defects and lessons for future projects. Independence and objective criteria make the review more informative than a celebration of deployment.',
'A project is declared successful because the software went live, although promised processing savings have not appeared.',
'Confusing installation with realized business benefit.',
'What would you measure after enough operating time has passed?',
'What is a primary purpose of a postimplementation review?',
'Evaluate whether expected business benefits and control objectives were achieved.',
'Repeat the vendor sales demonstration.','Guarantee that no future change is needed.','Replace all operational monitoring permanently.',
'The review tests outcomes against the original objectives and identifies improvements.'],

['d4-t01',
'IT components include compute, operating systems, storage, networks and supporting software. Audit understanding should connect these components to business services and failure points. A component inventory is useful only when dependencies and responsibilities are understood.',
'A critical service runs on two application servers but depends on one shared storage controller.',
'Counting redundant servers while overlooking their common dependency.',
'Which hidden shared component could still stop the entire service?',
'What most undermines the assumed resilience of two application servers?',
'A single shared component whose failure disables both.',
'Different application-server hostnames on the same network.','A documented maintenance schedule for both servers.','Two supported operating-system versions.',
'Redundancy must account for common failure points across the service, not only server count.'],
['d4-t02',
'Asset management tracks technology from acquisition through use, maintenance and disposal. Ownership, location, configuration and support status support risk decisions. Compare inventories with reliable discovery sources and investigate unmanaged assets.',
'A scan finds an unsupported server that is absent from the official inventory.',
'Assuming an inventory is complete because its existing records are accurate.',
'How would you detect assets missing from the approved inventory?',
'What most directly tests the completeness of an IT asset inventory?',
'Reconcile it with independent discovery and acquisition or disposal records.',
'Check only the spelling of listed hostnames.','Review only the accuracy of fields for already listed assets.','Ask one administrator whether anything is missing.',
'Independent sources can reveal unrecorded assets that a review of the inventory alone would miss.'],
['d4-t03',
'Scheduled jobs need authorized changes, dependency management, monitoring and controlled recovery. A job may finish successfully yet process incomplete input. Reconciliations and exception handling help establish that business processing actually completed.',
'An overnight payroll job runs before its input file arrives and reports technical success.',
'Treating a successful scheduler status as complete business processing.',
'What dependency and reconciliation would prevent this silent failure?',
'Which control best detects a successful job that processed no expected transactions?',
'Reconcile expected input and processing totals, with actionable exception alerts.',
'Review only the scheduler’s technical completion status.','Schedule the job more frequently without checking input.','Remove all job logs to reduce clutter.',
'Business-level reconciliation detects omissions that technical job status can miss.'],
['d4-t04',
'Interfaces move information between systems, so completeness, accuracy, authorization and timing matter. Sequence checks, acknowledgments, reconciliation and exception queues help detect lost or duplicated transactions. Document who resolves rejected records and how they are reprocessed.',
'An order is accepted by the sales system but rejected by the shipping interface without an alert.',
'Checking only transmission availability while ignoring end-to-end reconciliation.',
'How would you know whether every accepted order reached the next process?',
'Which control best supports interface completeness?',
'Reconcile sent, accepted and rejected records with owned exception resolution.',
'Only confirm that both systems are powered on.','Only compare the sender’s and receiver’s scheduled run times.','Allow rejected transactions to disappear automatically.',
'End-to-end totals and managed exceptions connect successful transmission to completed business processing.'],
['d4-t05',
'End-user computing and shadow IT can support business needs while creating ungoverned dependencies. Identify important spreadsheets, databases and services, assess their risk, and apply proportionate ownership, access, change and validation controls.',
'A spreadsheet outside IT calculates material commission payments using editable formulas.',
'Ignoring a business-critical tool because it is not a centrally developed application.',
'Which controls would matter if this spreadsheet makes an important business decision?',
'What should drive the level of control over an end-user spreadsheet?',
'Its business impact, complexity and risk.',
'Whether its creator works in IT.','Whether the file is stored in a department folder.','Whether it was built in one afternoon.',
'Control expectations should follow the exposure and significance of the process, not who wrote the tool.'],
['d4-t06',
'Availability concerns whether a service can be used when required; capacity concerns whether resources can support demand. Monitor trends, bottlenecks and agreed targets, and test realistic peak loads. Spare disk space does not establish adequate end-to-end capacity.',
'A service has ample storage but slows dramatically during month-end due to a database bottleneck.',
'Using one healthy resource metric as proof that the whole service can handle demand.',
'Which workload and dependency would you test to predict the peak user experience?',
'What is the best basis for capacity planning?',
'Business demand forecasts and observed end-to-end resource performance.',
'Only current free disk space.','Only the infrastructure utilization measured during quiet periods.','The assumption that last year’s demand never changes.',
'Capacity planning needs a demand view and an understanding of the resources that constrain service.'],
['d4-t07',
'Incident management restores service after disruption; problem management seeks underlying causes and prevents recurrence. A workaround can be a sensible immediate response while the root cause remains open for investigation.',
'Restarting a service restores operations every morning, but the same failure returns the next day.',
'Closing the underlying problem simply because a temporary workaround restores service.',
'What belongs in incident recovery, and what still requires problem analysis?',
'Which activity primarily belongs to problem management?',
'Investigating the underlying cause of recurring incidents.',
'Only notifying a user that service is restored.','Only applying the known temporary restart.','Communicating that the temporary restart restored the service.',
'Problem management focuses on root cause and recurrence reduction rather than immediate restoration alone.'],
['d4-t08',
'Changes should be authorized, evaluated, tested, implemented and verified with suitable segregation. Emergency changes still need controlled access, a justified expedited path and timely retrospective review. Patch prioritization should reflect exposure and business impact.',
'A critical patch is installed urgently, but no one later verifies what changed or whether approval was appropriate.',
'Treating an emergency as an exemption from every control.',
'Which controls can be expedited, and which evidence must still exist?',
'What should follow an expedited emergency production change?',
'Timely documented review and verification under the emergency-change process.',
'Automatic deletion of the change records.','A permanent exemption from future approvals.','Assumption that urgency proves the change was safe.',
'Emergency handling changes timing and routing, while accountability and verification remain necessary.'],
['d4-t09',
'Operational logs support monitoring and diagnosis when they capture relevant events and remain reliable. Time synchronization, restricted access, retention and review responsibilities help make events usable across systems. Logging without a response process can leave significant failures unnoticed.',
'Two systems record the same failure with clocks twenty minutes apart.',
'Collecting large log volumes without ensuring that events can be correlated or acted on.',
'What makes a log useful for reconstructing a cross-system event?',
'Which control most directly improves correlation of events across systems?',
'Consistent, monitored time synchronization.',
'Using different timestamp formats at random.','Removing timestamps from routine events.','Giving every user permission to edit logs.',
'Reliable aligned timestamps support event ordering and correlation across dependent systems.'],
['d4-t10',
'Service-level management translates business needs into measurable service commitments. Define measurement windows, responsibilities, reporting and escalation. Availability percentages alone can hide unacceptable disruption during a critical business period.',
'A provider meets monthly uptime even though its one outage repeatedly occurs during the daily payment deadline.',
'Assuming an aggregate service metric captures every business-critical requirement.',
'Which timing or outcome requirement should the agreement make explicit?',
'What best improves a service agreement for a time-critical payment process?',
'Targets and measurement that reflect the critical processing window.',
'Only a larger monthly report.','Only a promise of general best efforts.','Removing escalation responsibilities.',
'Service commitments should reflect the business consequence and timing of disruption.'],
['d4-t11',
'Database management protects reliable, authorized and recoverable data operations. Access, privileged changes, integrity constraints, performance, backup and recovery need coordinated controls. A successful backup is not proof that a consistent database can be restored.',
'A database backup completes daily, but no one has tested recovery of interdependent tables and transaction logs.',
'Assessing the backup job without considering database consistency and recovery requirements.',
'How would you demonstrate recovery to a valid, usable state?',
'What provides the strongest evidence that a database recovery approach works?',
'A tested restoration that meets consistency and recovery objectives.',
'Only a green backup-job status.','Only a large backup file.','Only a vendor brochure describing recovery features.',
'An actual restoration evaluates whether data can be returned to a consistent usable state.'],
['d4-t12',
'A business impact analysis examines how disruption affects critical processes over time. It identifies dependencies, recovery priorities and tolerable interruption or data loss. Business owners supply impact judgments; technology teams use those requirements to shape solutions.',
'IT chooses recovery targets based only on the speed of its existing backup product.',
'Treating current technical capability as the definition of business recovery need.',
'Who defines the impact, and how should that guide recovery priorities?',
'What should be the primary basis for recovery priorities?',
'Business impact and critical-process requirements.',
'Only the order in which servers were purchased.','Only the easiest systems to restore.','The infrastructure team’s preferred restoration order, without business validation.',
'Recovery priorities should follow the business consequence of disruption and the dependencies needed to resume work.'],
['d4-t13',
'Resilience is the ability to withstand, adapt to and recover from disruption. Redundancy is one technique, but common dependencies, operating procedures and tested failover matter. Consider people, suppliers, facilities and technology as one service.',
'Two data centers use different buildings but depend on the same upstream network path.',
'Counting alternate facilities without evaluating shared failure causes.',
'Which common dependency could defeat the apparent redundancy?',
'Which review best evaluates resilience of a critical service?',
'Assess end-to-end dependencies and test realistic disruption scenarios.',
'Count only the number of backup devices.','Assume different building addresses prove independence.','Review only the resilience policy’s publication date.',
'Resilience is demonstrated across the service and its dependencies, not by isolated redundancy claims.'],
['d4-t14',
'Backup creates recoverable copies; restoration makes those copies usable. Set coverage, retention, access and isolation to meet business recovery needs. Test restorations and protect backup administration so one compromised environment does not destroy every recovery copy.',
'Ransomware reaches both production and a continuously mounted backup location using the same credentials.',
'Assuming a copy is a resilient backup when it shares the original failure path.',
'What would stop the same event from damaging both production and recovery data?',
'Which design most directly reduces a common ransomware exposure to backups?',
'Protect isolated recovery copies and separate privileged backup access.',
'Use the same administrator password everywhere.','Keep only a continuously writable production mirror.','Disable restoration testing permanently.',
'Isolation and separate control of recovery copies reduce the chance that one compromise destroys all recovery options.'],
['d4-t15',
'A business continuity plan addresses how critical operations continue or resume during disruption. It includes people, communication, alternative procedures and dependencies as well as technology. Exercises should validate assumptions and feed improvements back into the plan.',
'The servers are restored, but payment staff cannot access the workplace or follow an alternate approval process.',
'Treating technical recovery as the entire continuity plan.',
'What people or business-process dependency would still stop operations?',
'Which gap most clearly shows an incomplete business continuity plan?',
'No workable alternate process for essential business activities.',
'The continuity plan uses a different document format from the recovery plan.','The plan identifies more than one authorized response coordinator.','The plan has both a printed and electronic copy.',
'Continuity must address the ability to perform critical business work, not only restore infrastructure.'],
['d4-t16',
'Disaster recovery translates business recovery requirements into technology restoration procedures. RTO concerns the target interruption duration; RPO concerns the point to which data should be recoverable. Test dependencies, sequence, staffing and results against those objectives.',
'A backup strategy can recover data from last night, but the business requires at most one hour of data loss.',
'Confusing how quickly a system returns with how much data can be lost.',
'Which objective describes downtime, and which describes data recency?',
'A process can tolerate four hours of downtime but only thirty minutes of lost data. Which requirement is the RPO?',
'Thirty minutes of potential data loss.',
'Four hours of downtime.','The number of recovery technicians.','The time required to restore the application service.',
'RPO describes the acceptable recovery point for data; RTO addresses the target time to restore service.'],

['d5-t01',
'An information-security framework organizes security objectives, responsibilities and control practices. Select and tailor controls to business risks and applicable requirements. Compliance mapping helps explain coverage, but adopting a framework name does not prove that controls work.',
'A company claims strong security because it has mapped policies to a framework, yet key controls are not operating.',
'Confusing documented alignment with demonstrated effectiveness.',
'What evidence would distinguish control design from actual operation?',
'What is needed beyond mapping controls to a recognized framework?',
'Evidence that suitable controls are implemented and operating effectively.',
'Only an approved policy-to-framework mapping.','Only a documented statement of security-program scope.','A rule that no control can be tailored to risk.',
'A framework helps structure the program; actual risk reduction depends on effective implementation and operation.'],
['d5-t02',
'Physical controls restrict inappropriate access; environmental controls protect operating conditions and facilities. Consider authorization, visitor handling, monitoring, fire protection, power and climate. Test control layers and exceptions rather than relying on one visible barrier.',
'A secured server room has badge access, but visitors can follow staff inside without challenge.',
'Assuming installed access technology proves that unauthorized entry is prevented.',
'Which behavior or bypass could defeat the physical access design?',
'What most directly addresses unauthorized visitors following staff through a secure door?',
'Effective visitor controls and anti-tailgating procedures supported by appropriate barriers.',
'Only an approved list of permanent staff with badge access.','Only a quarterly review of the room’s permanent access list.','Giving every visitor a permanent badge.',
'Physical access must manage real entry paths and behavior, including bypass of an authorized badge check.'],
['d5-t03',
'Identity and access management establishes who a user is and what they may do. Use approved access, least privilege, segregation, timely joiner-mover-leaver updates and periodic review. Authentication proves an identity claim; authorization limits its permitted actions.',
'A transferred employee keeps purchasing approval rights that their new role no longer needs.',
'Thinking a successful MFA login proves that the user’s permissions are appropriate.',
'What should change when a person’s business responsibilities change?',
'What best addresses inappropriate access retained after a job transfer?',
'Review and adjust authorization to the employee’s new responsibilities.',
'Only confirm that the transferred employee still has an active account.','Only confirm the password is complex.','Assume MFA removes the need for access review.',
'Role changes require authorization changes; strong authentication does not justify excessive privileges.'],
['d5-t04',
'Network and endpoint security uses layers to limit exposure, restrict communication and detect compromise. Segmentation should follow trust and business needs. Harden and monitor endpoints because a protected network boundary does not make internal devices inherently trustworthy.',
'A compromised workstation can reach production administration interfaces because internal traffic is broadly trusted.',
'Assuming all traffic inside a network perimeter is legitimate.',
'Which communication actually needs to be permitted for this business role?',
'Which measure most directly reduces unnecessary lateral access from employee workstations?',
'Segmentation and explicit restrictions on permitted administration paths.',
'Only patch the workstations without restricting their network access.','Giving all employees shared administrator credentials.','Disabling internal traffic logs.',
'Limiting reachable services and administrative paths reduces opportunities to move from one compromised endpoint to critical systems.'],
['d5-t05',
'Data loss prevention uses classification and monitoring or enforcement to reduce inappropriate data disclosure. Coverage may include endpoints, networks and cloud services. Tune policies, manage exceptions and assess bypass paths; alert volume alone does not establish effective prevention.',
'A DLP tool blocks large email attachments but sensitive data can still be uploaded to unmanaged cloud storage.',
'Equating one protected channel with complete data-loss coverage.',
'Which data movements remain outside the current control?',
'What should an auditor assess when evaluating DLP coverage?',
'Important data types, movement paths, policy actions and managed exceptions.',
'Only the number of generated alerts.','Only the license cost.','Only the documented approval of the monitoring policy.',
'Coverage depends on where sensitive data moves and how the control responds in those channels.'],
['d5-t06',
'Encryption protects confidentiality when data is transformed using appropriate algorithms and keys. Key generation, storage, access, rotation, backup and revocation are essential. Encryption does not by itself establish proper authorization or protect data after an authorized decryption.',
'A database is encrypted, but its decryption key is stored in a file accessible to every application user.',
'Evaluating the encryption algorithm while ignoring how the key is protected.',
'Who can obtain the key, and what could they decrypt with it?',
'What most directly undermines strong encryption at rest?',
'Inappropriate access to the decryption key.',
'A documented encryption configuration baseline.','A documented key owner.','A tested procedure for recovering an authorized key backup.',
'Anyone who can obtain and use the key may bypass the confidentiality benefit of encryption.'],
['d5-t07',
'Public key infrastructure binds identities to public keys through certificates and trusted authorities. Trust depends on issuance validation, private-key protection, certificate status and lifecycle handling. A valid signature is useful only when the certificate and trust path are appropriately verified.',
'An application accepts a certificate without checking whether its issuer is trusted or the certificate has been revoked.',
'Treating possession of any certificate as proof of a trustworthy identity.',
'What checks connect a certificate to a currently trusted identity?',
'What is essential when relying on a certificate to authenticate a service?',
'Validate its trust chain, intended identity, validity and relevant status.',
'Accept it solely because the certificate was supplied by the remote service.','Check only the certificate’s expiration date.','Share the service’s private key with all users.',
'Certificate trust requires validation of the identity binding and its current trusted status, not just the presence of a certificate.'],
['d5-t08',
'Cloud and virtualized services distribute responsibilities across provider and customer. Determine the service model, contractual commitments and control boundaries. The customer still needs appropriate identity, configuration, data handling and assurance over the responsibilities it retains.',
'A SaaS customer assumes the provider’s security report proves that every customer user has appropriate privileges.',
'Confusing provider assurance with assurance over customer-controlled configuration and access.',
'Which responsibilities belong to the provider, and which remain with this customer?',
'What should be evaluated first when assigning cloud control responsibilities?',
'The service model and the agreed shared-responsibility boundary.',
'Only the provider’s brand recognition.','An assumption that all controls transfer to the provider.','Only the provider’s reported aggregate uptime.',
'The control boundary determines which party implements each safeguard and what assurance the customer needs.'],
['d5-t09',
'Mobile, wireless and IoT devices expand the attack surface through remote access, physical loss, weak defaults and uneven update support. Inventory devices, manage access and configuration, and isolate less-trusted devices according to their purpose and exposure.',
'Building sensors use default credentials and share a network with finance workstations.',
'Ignoring connected devices because they are not conventional employee computers.',
'What could this device access if its weak credential is compromised?',
'Which action most directly limits the effect of a compromised building sensor?',
'Isolate its network access to required services and remove insecure defaults.',
'Give it the same privileges as finance administrators.','Stop recording which devices are connected.','Assume a small device cannot create significant risk.',
'Purpose-limited connectivity and secure configuration reduce the paths from a weak device to higher-impact assets.'],
['d5-t10',
'Awareness programs help people recognize risks and act appropriately. Tailor training to roles, reinforce it over time and assess behavior as well as completion. A useful reporting culture lets staff escalate suspected events promptly without hiding mistakes.',
'Every employee finishes annual training, but staff routinely send suspicious messages to colleagues instead of reporting them.',
'Treating course completion as proof that safe behavior has changed.',
'What behavior would show that the learning is being used?',
'Which measure best evaluates whether phishing-awareness training influences behavior?',
'Appropriate reporting and response behavior in realistic exercises.',
'Only the number of training modules made available.','Only the total attendance count.','Only employee self-reports that the training was easy to understand.',
'Behavioral evidence is more informative than exposure to training material alone.'],
['d5-t11',
'Attack methods exploit technical weaknesses, trust or human behavior to compromise confidentiality, integrity or availability. Understand the path from initial access to impact so controls address relevant steps. Do not infer the full impact from a single alert without investigation.',
'An attacker obtains a staff password through a fake login page and then uses excessive permissions to export records.',
'Focusing only on the phishing entry point while overlooking authorization and data monitoring.',
'Which additional control could limit harm after an account is compromised?',
'Which control most directly limits the impact of a stolen ordinary-user account?',
'Least-privilege authorization and restrictions on sensitive actions.',
'Allowing every user unrestricted exports.','Relying only on the account’s original approval.','Assuming an authenticated action cannot be malicious.',
'Reducing what a compromised account can do limits impact even when initial access succeeds.'],
['d5-t12',
'Security testing should have authorization, clear scope and a method suited to the objective. Vulnerability scanning identifies potential weaknesses; penetration testing examines exploitability within agreed constraints. Validate findings and manage production safety and remediation follow-up.',
'A scanner labels a service vulnerable based only on its reported version, which may include a backported fix.',
'Equating every tool finding with a confirmed exploitable weakness.',
'What additional evidence would support this tool-generated conclusion?',
'What should happen before a scanner finding becomes a confirmed reportable exposure?',
'Validate the condition and assess relevant exploitability and business impact.',
'Assume all scanner results are false.','Report the maximum severity without review.','Run disruptive tests without authorization.',
'Validation distinguishes a possible weakness from a supported risk conclusion while preserving authorized testing boundaries.'],
['d5-t13',
'Security monitoring connects relevant events to detection logic, investigation and response. Reliable timestamps, useful context and protected logs support correlation. Tune rules and escalation based on risk; a large collection of unreviewed alerts can hide meaningful signals.',
'A monitoring platform generates thousands of alerts each day, but no team owns triage or escalation.',
'Using alert volume or tool deployment as proof of effective detection.',
'Who will act on this signal, and how will they judge its significance?',
'Which missing element most undermines a deployed monitoring platform?',
'An accountable process to triage, investigate and escalate relevant alerts.',
'An approved log-retention schedule with no investigation responsibility.','A periodic inventory of the monitoring platform’s installed agents.','A higher count of duplicated alerts.',
'Detection has little operational value if relevant events do not lead to timely investigation and response.'],
['d5-t14',
'Incident response coordinates detection, analysis, containment, eradication and recovery, with communication and learning across the process. Prepare roles and decision authority before an event. Immediate actions should balance impact, evidence preservation and the risks of continued compromise.',
'A team disconnects an affected host to limit spread, then needs to preserve evidence and coordinate recovery.',
'Treating response as an improvised technical fix without ownership or communication.',
'Which decisions require coordinated authority rather than an individual administrator’s assumption?',
'What most improves the organization’s ability to respond consistently to a serious security incident?',
'A rehearsed response plan with defined roles, escalation and decision authority.',
'Only the deployment of additional monitoring products without defined response roles.','An assumption that the first person to notice the event owns every decision.','A rule that incidents should never be discussed.',
'Preparation and rehearsal help coordinate technical actions, business decisions and communications under pressure.'],
['d5-t15',
'Forensic work preserves and examines evidence so findings remain defensible. Record collection, handling, transfers and integrity checks. Use appropriate acquisition methods and authorized specialists; changing the original system casually can destroy information or weaken reliability.',
'A technician investigates suspected misuse by editing files directly on the only original drive without recording the steps.',
'Prioritizing curiosity over preservation and a documented chain of custody.',
'How could another examiner establish that the evidence is authentic and unchanged?',
'Which practice best supports the defensibility of digital evidence?',
'Documented preservation, integrity verification and chain of custody.',
'Unrecorded edits to the only original media.','Sharing evidence through personal accounts without tracking.','Assuming a screenshot proves how all files were handled.',
'A documented evidence trail and integrity controls support confidence in what was collected and how it was examined.']
];

export const CISA_MICROLESSONS = Object.freeze(rows.map((row,index)=>{
  const [id,concept,scenario,trap,retrievalPrompt,question,answer,...rest]=row;
  const rationale=rest.pop(),all=[answer,...rest];
  const offset=index%4,choiceTexts=all.slice(4-offset).concat(all.slice(0,4-offset));
  const choices=Object.fromEntries(choiceTexts.map((text,i)=>['ABCD'[i],text]));
  const lesson=LESSONS.find(l=>l.id===id);
  if(!lesson)throw new Error(`Unknown micro-lesson ${id}`);
  return Object.freeze({id,lessonId:id,title:lesson.title,domainId:lesson.domainId,domain:lesson.domain,
    minutes:2,estimatedSeconds:90,concept,explanation:concept,scenario,trap,retrievalPrompt,
    sourceIds:['isaca-outline','isaca-glossary',...(lesson.domainId==='d2'?['nist-csf']:lesson.domainId==='d5'?['nist-controls']:[])],
    checkpoint:Object.freeze({id:`micro_${id}`,question,choices,correctAnswer:Object.keys(choices).find(k=>choices[k]===answer),rationale,source:'ControlQuest original checkpoint'}),
    original:true,official:false});
}));
export function getMicroLesson(id){return CISA_MICROLESSONS.find(lesson=>lesson.id===id)||null;}
export function microLessonsForDomain(domainId){return CISA_MICROLESSONS.filter(lesson=>!domainId||lesson.domainId===domainId);}
export function microLessonSpeechText(id){const lesson=getMicroLesson(id);return lesson?`${lesson.title}. ${lesson.concept} Example. ${lesson.scenario} Common trap. ${lesson.trap} Pause and recall. ${lesson.retrievalPrompt}`:'';}
